.sass-cache/
node_modules
package-lock.json

# Client work carries names, pricing, terms and schedules. Kept out of the
# repository — the design and the tooling are the reusable asset, not the
# documents built from them.
content/proposal-*/
content/checklist-*/
content/client-*/
# Built books from client manuscripts. The theme, the passes and the style map
# are the asset and belong here; the author's text does not. `content/aet-raw/`
# and `content/aet-tail/` predate this rule and are still tracked — see the
# note in CLAUDE.md about client material.
# Any built book from a client manuscript, whatever it is called this week.
# A narrow list of slugs is how a 4 MB copy of the author's text ended up in
# the branch twice: the rule has to cover the next name too, not the last one.
content/aet*/
content/book-*/
content/client-*/
# Slices of a manuscript cut for timing a render. Same rule, same reason.
content/test-*/
# Debug reproductions. A Paged.js fault that will not show in a synthetic page
# shows at once in a region cut out of the real book, so cutting one out is the
# right move — but what comes out is the author's text, and `git add -A` does
# not know that. 116 KB of the manuscript reached the branch this way, in a
# commit about something else entirely. Whatever the scratch directory is
# called, it goes here first.
content/repro*/
content/spantest*/
content/ack-tmp/
content/scratch-*/


# Deal files carry client names, fees and terms. The template they fill in is
# committed; the values are not. `proposals/example-deal.json` is the exception.
.claude/skills/book-format/proposals/deals/


# Python bytecode, which has been turning up as a change on every run
__pycache__/
*.pyc
